---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import CaseStudyCard from '../components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import explorerShot from '../assets/intelligence-explorer.png';
import variantCoverage from '../assets/p2p-variant-coverage.png';
import gbsTornado from '../assets/gbs-tornado.png';
import daxAnswer from '../assets/dax-intelligence-answer.png';
import agenticMix from '../assets/gbs-agentic-mix.png';
import fpaForecastError from '../assets/fpa-forecast-error.png';
import fccaControlReview from '../assets/fcca-control-review.png';

// Ordered as an engagement runs: diagnose the operation, read how the model is
// changing, quantify the case, build the tooling, forecast what happens next,
// then put controls around what the tooling is allowed to decide.
// The Intelligence Agent closes the set — it is the one that has run
// unattended for months rather than the one that produced a finding.
const caseStudies = [
  {
    title: 'P2P Process Mining',
    problem:
      'Every finance transformation deck opens with the same sentence — the process is inefficient and highly manual. Almost nobody can say by how much, or where.',
    build:
      'Mined a 1.6M-event SAP purchase-to-pay log covering 251,734 purchase order line items, reconstructing the path each case actually took rather than the one the process documentation describes. Variant analysis, straight-through rate, the cycle-time cost of rework, and which sequence rules the system genuinely enforces versus merely documents.',
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'LLM API', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '20%',
    metricLabel: 'of cases follow the most common path; rework costs 19.6 days',
    implication:
      'Cycle time here is an exception problem, not a capacity problem — and the expensive exceptions are not the frequent ones. A programme that targets the loudest rework activity would pick one of the cheapest. Prioritisation has to follow volume times penalty, which is only visible once the log is mined.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/p2p-process-mining',
    figure: {
      src: variantCoverage,
      alt: 'Cumulative share of cases covered as more process variants are included, on a log-scaled rank axis. The curve rises steeply then flattens: 50% of cases at rank 7, 90% at rank 232, 99% only at rank 9,456.',
      caption:
        'Coverage against variant rank. Half the cases sit in seven variants; the last percent needs nine thousand more.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Agentic Shift',
    problem:
      'McKinsey argues agentic AI is turning the GBS talent pyramid into a diamond — a shrinking transactional base and a new layer managing the agent force. It is a claim about what work looks like, cited far more often than it is checked.',
    build:
      "Read the claim off the labour market instead. Classified 2,110 live GBS and finance-operations postings across ten markets as transactional, judgment or agent-ops — a visible phrase taxonomy carries the headline, an LLM decides only the ambiguous residual, and the classifier's accuracy is measured against a hand-labelled gold set rather than asserted. Splitting by employer is the sharpest cut: captive GBS hires 38% transactional, third-party providers 84%. The base changed employer more than it shrank.",
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'LLM API', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '2%',
    metricLabel: 'of postings ask for agent-ops skills — the diamond is not visible yet',
    implication:
      'Workforce plans built on the diamond thesis are ahead of the evidence. The live decision is not agent-ops recruitment but sourcing: a captive function that looks transformed may simply have moved its transactional base to a provider, and only the employer cut separates the two.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-agentic-shift',
    figure: {
      src: agenticMix,
      alt: 'A single stacked bar across 2,110 GBS job postings: transactional 44%, judgment 55%, and a thin agent-ops sliver at 2% annotated in orange at the right edge.',
      caption:
        'Ten markets, one point-in-time cross-section. A measured 42.9% recall on the agent-ops class makes that 2% a lower bound rather than a ceiling — the classifier reports how wrong it is, so the finding can be read honestly.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Business Case',
    problem:
      'A shared-services business case usually rests on numbers guessed in a workshop — automation rate, rework share, handling time — presented as if they were measured.',
    build:
      'Built the case on the P2P Process Mining baseline instead: opportunity sizing and discounted cash flows from the measured straight-through rate and rework cost, then a 10,000-draw Monte Carlo across every assumption still uncertain. Each one is tagged whether diligence could resolve it or genuinely cannot yet, so the variance in the answer sorts into a work plan rather than a caveat.',
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'NumPy', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '62%',
    metricLabel: 'of NPV variance comes from assumptions diligence can resolve — the work plan, not the caveat',
    implication:
      'At central assumptions the case does not clear the hurdle rate, which is a result rather than a dead end. The uncertainty that decides it is a handling-time study, not a vendor quote — so a week of desk observation moves the answer further than another round of estimating, and the model says which week to spend.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-business-case',
    figure: {
      src: gbsTornado,
      alt: 'Horizontal tornado chart of NPV sensitivity by assumption, low value to high value, bars coloured by whether diligence could measure the assumption or not. Minutes per rework touch, a measurable assumption, produces the widest swing — ahead of the implementation one-off cost.',
      caption:
        'The widest swing comes from a measurable assumption, not the implementation cost quote — the opposite of what a workshop case usually assumes.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'DAX Intelligence',
    problem:
      'Answering one comparative question across a set of annual reports means opening every PDF and trusting your memory of what each one said.',
    build:
      'Retrieval across 15 DAX 40 annual reports. ChromaDB surfaces the relevant passages and the LLM synthesises them behind a prompt that will not answer without a source, so every claim comes back with a company and a page number.',
    stack: ['Python', 'ChromaDB', 'LLM API', 'Streamlit'],
    metricValue: '15 reports',
    metricLabel: 'answerable in one query, with page citations',
    implication:
      'Analyst time shifts from finding a passage to judging it. The citation check is what makes that shift safe: an answer carrying a verified page number can go into a client document as it stands, while an unsourced one has to be found by hand anyway — which is the work it was supposed to save.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/dax-intelligence',
    figure: {
      src: daxAnswer,
      alt: 'A synthesised research memo comparing how Siemens and SAP frame AI investment strategy, each claim followed by an inline (Company Year, p.N) citation. Below it a green "VERIFIED — 8/8 citations matched retrieved excerpts exactly" badge, an export button, and the start of an audit trail table listing each retrieved excerpt with its page range and confidence score.',
      caption:
        'A real query against the 2025 filings. Every claim carries a page citation, and every citation is checked against the retrieved excerpt before it reaches the screen.',
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'FP&A Decision Intelligence Accelerator',
    problem:
      'FP&A review cycles reliably produce analysis and much less reliably produce decisions. The pack lands, the variances are explained, the sensitivity table is admired — and the meeting ends without anyone naming which exposure is worth the next thirty minutes, who owns it, or what would have to change for the answer to move.',
    build:
      "A driver-based forecast for adidas, built from published filings, backtested against two vintages: FY2023\u2192FY2024 and FY2024\u2192FY2025, each using the initial guidance from the prior year's report rather than a figure revised part-way through the year it describes. On top of it, a decision layer. Each driver's financial exposure is computed by re-running the forecast across its plausible range, then ranked against two declared judgements — how firm the assumption is, and whether management can move it inside the year. Low controllability caps a driver at Monitor however large it is, because attention is a budget and should be spent where it converts. The engine is separated from the client: drivers, scenarios, thresholds and mappings live in configuration, a second client with materially different economics runs on the same code, and a third can be stood up from a spreadsheet without touching it.",
    stack: ['Python', 'NumPy', 'FastAPI', 'Next.js', 'LLM API', 'pytest', 'Vitest'],
    metricValue: '\u20ac900m',
    metricLabel:
      'unforecast working-capital release in FY2024 \u2014 the same assumption then cost \u20ac372m in FY2025, making it the largest error contributor in both years, in opposite directions',
    implication:
      "The ranking and the backtest agree, and neither was tuned to the other: the driver the materiality engine puts first for management attention is the one that has actually broken the forecast, twice. The second vintage also falsified a claim this project had been making. Carrying FY2023's 189% effective tax rate forward \u2014 tax on a near-zero pre-tax result in the write-off year \u2014 made the model look worse than a naive extrapolation on cash. That was the model being wrong, not the method being weak; the rule is guarded now, and the correction is in the repository history rather than quietly removed.",
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/fpa-decision-intelligence',
    demoUrl: 'https://fpa-decision-intelligence.vercel.app/priorities',
    figure: {
      src: fpaForecastError,
      alt: 'Two stacked bar charts on a shared scale, one per backtest vintage. FY2023 to FY2024: naive extrapolation errs 13.9% on revenue, 84.8% on operating profit and 72.2% on free cash flow, against 5.0%, 62.6% and 54.7% for the driver-based forecast. FY2024 to FY2025: naive errs 5.5%, 22.3% and 14.8%, against 3.1%, 14.9% and 3.4%. The driver-based bar is shorter in all six, and the FY2024 misses are several times the FY2025 ones.',
      caption:
        'Two vintages, not a track record. The driver-based forecast lands closer on all six metric-year pairs \u2014 a statement about a weak baseline, not about accuracy, since both methods undershot in both years and the FY2024 operating-profit forecast was wrong by 63%.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'Finance Close Control Agent',
    problem:
      'A month-end close raises more exceptions than there are reviewer hours, and most of them are benign. Handing the triage to a model is an obvious idea and a dangerous one: a control has to run the same checks on every item, rest on a policy someone can open and read, and survive an auditor asking six months later what the system knew when it made a recommendation.',
    build:
      'Eighteen deterministic checks run over the ledger in DuckDB before a model sees anything — materiality, approval, segregation of duties, duplicates, reconciliation status. LlamaIndex then retrieves the governing policy sections with document and clause preserved, and the model classifies and recommends inside a closed vocabulary. Every citation it returns is matched against what was actually retrieved and stripped if it was not, and a deterministic gate — not the model — decides whether a person must look. Benchmarked over 60 labelled exceptions on AWS Bedrock, with the whole decision reconstructable from an audit record.',
    stack: ['Python', 'LangChain', 'LlamaIndex', 'AWS Bedrock', 'DuckDB'],
    metricValue: '0 missed',
    metricLabel: 'escalations across 60 exceptions — on two models and a repeat run, at a cost of 7 to 11 false ones',
    implication:
      'The direction of the error is the finding. Benign items reached a reviewer who did not need to see them; nothing that required review was cleared. That held when the model was swapped for one costing a third as much — risk accuracy fell from 0.87 to 0.70 and missed escalations stayed at zero — and again when the same run was repeated. Judgement is the model\'s and degrades with its price; the guarantee is the gate\'s and does not.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/finance-close-control-agent',
    figure: {
      src: fccaControlReview,
      alt: 'A control review for one exception: a table of the four deterministic checks that fired with their severity, observed value and detail; the risk rating, finding and recommended action; a disposition of HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED with the three reasons that forced it; and four cited policy sections with relevance scores and source file paths.',
      caption:
        'One exception decided end to end on AWS Bedrock. The checks that fired carry their own thresholds, the disposition names the rule that forced it, and every policy section is quotable back to a file. Real output, typeset; two blocks abridged where marked.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Intelligence Agent',
    problem:
      'Competitor and client intelligence reached the team through whoever happened to read the right article — usually weeks after it could have changed a proposal.',
    build:
      'A pipeline that reads 69 feeds across 33 consulting firms, analyst houses and client companies, then has the LLM score every article 0–3 for strategic relevance. Only the top tier reaches the Monday brief; everything below the cut stays searchable in a dashboard.',
    stack: ['Python', 'LLM API', 'SQLite'],
    metricValue: '16 weeks',
    metricLabel: 'delivered end to end, no manual step',
    implication:
      'Market awareness stops depending on who happened to read what. The summarising is the least of it — the point is that the filter runs on a Monday whether or not anyone remembers to look, which is the difference between a tool that was built and one that is used.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-intelligence-agent',
    signal: { total: 1132, signal: 30 },
    figure: {
      src: explorerShot,
      alt: 'The Intelligence Explorer dashboard: a filter bar above a grid of article cards, each carrying a source logo, topic tags and a relevance badge. Text is obscured.',
      caption:
        'The Intelligence Explorer — one card per signal, grouped by cluster, filtered by topic and calendar week. Content obscured; the live dashboard carries competitive intelligence.',
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Hero />
  <Section id="case-studies" heading="Case Studies">
    {caseStudies.map((cs) => <CaseStudyCard {...cs} />)}
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
